{"text":"Recent attention research supports explicitly modeling temporal relationships. [PARec/FPARec (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.02793) learns positional relations directly, motivated by evidence that learned position embeddings largely encode token distance. [ScaleRec (2026)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ipm.2025.104318) models transient interests and stable preferences through multiscale attention and adaptive masking. [T-RoPE (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30576) explores timestamp-based rotary angles and learnable temporal scales for generative recommendation. These offer transferable attention ideas, rather than direct evidence of gains within BERT4Rec.

Text representation work highlights both opportunity and fusion difficulty. [Sequence-level Semantic Representation Fusion (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.18166) argues that text and item-ID features have different characteristics, making direct embedding addition less effective. [ITS-Rec (2025)](https://www.mdpi.com/2079-9292/14/9/1748) combines titles, descriptions and reviews using BERT-derived representations, but its pretrained features, review dependence and SASRec backbone prevent direct adoption under this task’s constraints. These results do not establish that equivalent benefits survive training text representations from scratch.

A compatible experimental hypothesis: retain one masked-item BERT4Rec; jointly learn item-ID embeddings and shared title/description token or subword embeddings, with a small compositional text encoder and learned fusion gate. Add relative-position and bucketed observed-time-gap attention biases; test recent-window attention heads within the same network. Preserve appended-mask inference, exposing neither target identity/text nor its unavailable timestamp. Ablate text composition, temporal bias and recent-context attention separately using macro validation HR@10. Complementarity and satisfied needs remain unobserved hypotheses; semantic similarity and interaction timing alone cannot establish either mechanism."}
